INT. APARTMENT - KITCHEN - EVENING

A small kitchen. A table set for two. One candle burning. A phone leans against the salt shaker.

CLEMENTINE BRAITHWAITE, 30s, in an apron, stirs a pot. She tastes. Adds salt. Checks the clock. She wipes her hands, straightens a fork, moves it a quarter inch, moves it back.

The phone buzzes. She answers on speaker. Only a long silence and a car horn on the other end.

CLEMENTINE
It's fine.

She waits.

CLEMENTINE (CONT'D)
No, I'm stirring. Twenty is fine.

The line clicks dead. She does not look at the phone. A moment later it rings again: her own voicemail greeting, played back by mistake.

CLEMENTINE (V.O.)
(recorded, cheerful)
Hi, you've reached Clementine. Leave a message and I'll call you right back.

She presses the screen. The voice stops. She takes the second plate from the table and slides it back into the cupboard. She blows out the candle. Sits. Eats alone, slowly, while the pot goes on simmering.
